  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...A front will drop through the forecast area \nthis afternoon, bringing rain showers and isolated \nthunderstorms to the region.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\nA cold front will cross the forecast area late this afternoon.\nShowers are developing across the CWA in a few different areas\nbut have not gotten tall enough to produce lightning. A few\nareas of thunderstorms are in Quebec and will pivot SE into\nMaine over the next 1-2hrs. Though 0-6 km shear is quite high, \napproaching 40-50 kt, CAPE is on the lower end, around 300 J/kg\nfrom the 18z CAR Upper Air Sounding. RAP modeling suggesting up\nto 500 J/kg ahead of the cold front. There will not be any deep\nlayer of moisture to work with. 18z sounding from CAR shows a\nvery dry and warmer mid level layer that is limiting storms from\ndeveloping tall enough. However, expecting storms to get tall\nenough to produce lightning. 18z CAR upper air does show a well\nmixed boundary layer (inverted V signature) up to 780mb. Lines \nof training storms could develop along the front and prefrontal \nboundaries themselves as they cross the area, and this would be \nthe best chance at any stronger organization for enhanced \nthreats, but even then, at most it appears maybe some gusty \nwinds with these storms based on the lack of extensive severe \nparameters being met at this time.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...System brings round of rain showers mid-to-late\nweek.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\nHigh pressure over northern New England early week, as a low \npressure moves eastward through Canada. Chance for some stray \nshowers moving into the Central Highlands and Bangor regions on\nTuesday, as some models hint at a disturbance developing off of\nthe aforementioned parent low in Canada. \n\nFor rain mid-to-late week, global models are trending towards \nthe low pressure system moving through Maine Wednesday night \ninto Thursday. The disagreement between models is currently in \na surface low that develops off of the parent low in Canada. The\nGFS has a surface low develop on Thursday, and staying out in \nthe Atlantic. The 00-06z ECMWF, however, has a surface low \ndevelop much earlier on Tuesday, over the Gulf of Maine, and \nslowly move out to sea through the week. With either solution, \nanticipating highest rain totals along the Downeast region. \nConfidence in timing, severe potential, and rain amounts, will \nbegin to improve once models get a better handle on the \nenvironment for next week.",
